# The sum of two intervals of the round-up interval (docs/developer/design/upward.md, "Sum,
# difference, negation"): a state (u, n) is the upper bound and the negated lower bound, and one
# addition of the two registers, each lane rounded toward +infinity, gives RU of the sum of the upper
# bounds and RU of the sum of the negated lower bounds, minus RD of the sum of the lower bounds: the
# tightest interval of T. An empty operand has a NaN in both lanes, and so has the result. Requires
# the rounding mode upward set by kairn::upward::Scope and the options of docs/concepts/upward.md
# (GCC and Clang -frounding-math, MSVC /fp:strict).
kernel:        upward_add_1
rounding:      upward
input:         pair stored x = xu xn
input:         pair stored y = yu yn
output:        pair z = zu zn
requires:      upward xu xn
requires:      upward yu yn
ensures:       encloses_interval zu zn x+y x=xu,xn y=yu,yn tight=rounded bounded_when=abs_le(x+y,omega) bounded_exactly
min_precision: 1
source:        the sums of the lanes rounded upward (the listing of the round-up interval)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_only:     yes
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeUpwardArith.v upward_add_1_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   each lane is a bound of its own, and a lane that is not finite is part of a state (an unbounded interval, or with the other lane the empty set); encloses_interval states what comes out

zu = add_ru(xu, yu) | zn = add_ru(xn, yn)       # x + y
